<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<!--/* Rev AA 04-07-2023 */-->
<head>
  <title>NDFIMG: Transform NDF raster format files (satellite images, etc) to
  MiraMon IMG format</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>

<body>
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.--> 
  <p>
  "NATIONAL LAND ARCHIVE PRODUCTION SYSTEM (NLAPS). SYSTEMATIC FORMAT
  DESCRIPTION DOCUMENT". United States Geological Survey Format
  Specifications for Geometrically Corrected Landsat Level 1 Digital Data
  Products (July 2000)<br/>
  <br/>
  "Level 1 Product Output Files. Data Format Control Book". Volume
  5, Book 2. Revision 5. April 2001<br/></p>
  
  <p>The images are converted to MiraMon IMG v. 4 format, so the different
  spectral bands are stored in as many IMG files as there are spectral bands
  the original image. All the converted IMG files are stored in the same
  directory, so the user is asked to enter a directory name rather than an
  output file name for each spectral band. The program compresses the images
  into RLE extracompressed format.</p>

  <p>The names of the IMG files that will contain the different spectral bands
  or channels are assigned according to the following pattern:
  orbit&amp;image_date_Nband.img. An example could be: 197031_13062002_1
  corresponding to the orbit 197, image 031, taken 13 June 2002 in spectral
  channel 1 (blue).</p>

  <p>In case of floating images, the NDF heading files indicate the scene of
  beginning and end with a decimal figure. This information is used to obtain
  the scene number identifying of the scene, from the average rounded to
  integer of the initial and final scene number.</p>

  <p>For instance, if the Report file (*.WO) says:</p>
  <pre>
   Path/Strip no.:     197                        Start Row no.:      030.9
                                                  End Row no.:        031.9
</pre>the names of the different bands will follow the pattern:
197031_13062002_?.

  <p>The metadata contained in the files that are usually found on an
  ETM+/TM/MSS CD-ROM, like for example the date and hour of acquisition,
  preprocessing details, etc, are stored in the I.rel file in the same
  directory where the images are stored. This multiband metadata file
  distinguishes between the data specific to each band (such as the spectral
  range covered), from the data common to all bands (like the acquisition
  date) and those data which relate to both (like for instance the number of
  columns and rows, which is the same for all the multispectral bands but is
  usually double for the panchromatic band of ETM+ images).</p>

  <p>The different bands are found in the metadata file in the following
  order:</p>

  <div style="margin-left: 2em">
    * <u>ETM+ and TM</u>

    <div style="margin-left: 2em">
      <p>- First the PAN image (band 8), as it has the highest spatial
      resolution and is unique in the set. This band is only available with
      the ETM+ sensor and not TM.</p>
	</div>
	
	<div style="margin-left: 2em">
      <p>-Then the bands 1-2-3-4 that provide the spectral response in the
      ranges blue - green - red - near infrared.</p>
	  </div>

	<div style="margin-left: 2em">
      <p>-Then the bands 5-7, that provide the response in the middle infrared
      spectral region.</p>
	  </div>

	<div style="margin-left: 2em">
      <p>- Finally band 6, corresponding to the thermal infrared that has a
      resolution of 60 m in ETM+ and 120 m in TM, but is distributed resampled
      to 30 m. In the case of ETM+ images, sometimes these are distributed
      with an additional band 9, corresponding to the thermal channel but with
      a high gain radiometric calibration.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-left: 2em">
    * <u>MSS</u>

    <div style="margin-left: 2em">
      - The bands 1-2-3-4-5 that provide the spectral response in the ranges
      green - red - near infrared 1- near infrared 2 - thermic. Band 5 is
      only available with the mission Landsat-3 of MSS sensor.
    </div>
  </div>The MiraMon Metadata Manager (GeMM) may be used to make any changes or
  minor modifications as needed after importing the images.
  <hr/>

<exclusiva>
